  planner_response {
    thinking: "**Designing Piece Tracking**\n\nAlright, I\'m working on the piece tracking and motion transition architecture for `ChessBoard.tsx`. I need to ensure that when any move happens, whether from the user, AI, or elsewhere, the piece animates smoothly from its origin to its destination. I plan to use Framer Motion, specifically a motion spring, to achieve this.\n\n\n**Refining Animations And Interactions**\n\nI\'ve been further refining the motion transitions. For castling, the King and Rook will slide in sync. Captured pieces will gracefully scale down and fade out. Pawn promotion will involve a smooth slide to the 8th rank. I am making sure the drag-and-drop interactions remain responsive. When dragging, the underlying board piece is hidden, and when dropped, the pieces land cleanly.\n\n\n**Implementing Visual Features**\n\nI am now focusing on implementing visual features to enhance the game experience. I\'m prioritizing the integration of board highlights: selected squares, legal move indicators, and last move highlights. I\'m experimenting with different color schemes and styles for these elements, considering options like AoT green, Batman yellow, and Peshmerga gold. I\'m also planning to implement a king-in-check danger aura. I am confident these elements will provide more clarity to the board.\n\n\n**Tracking Piece Movement**\n\nI\'m now focusing on piece movement tracking with the `lastMove` data. I\'m working through the logic to match pieces between board states efficiently. When there is no previous state, or there is no last move, or the board resets, I assign unique IDs to each piece. When a last move does exist, I\'ll identify the moving piece from `lastMove.from` to `lastMove.to` and then match it to its previous state.\n\n\n**Matching Piece Identities**\n\nThe piece\'s `square` is updated based on `lastMove.to` (and `type` if promoted), yet maintains its original `id`. I\'m working on the castling special case, matching both the king and the rook based on their respective movements. Finally, I identify stationary pieces based on color and type in the previous board state. Captured pieces are naturally excluded.\n\n\n**Finalizing Piece Transitions**\n\nNow, I\'ve implemented the `AnimatePresence` exit animation for removed pieces. New or unmatched pieces get a fresh unique ID. I\'m storing the new piece list in `previousPiecesRef.current` and updating the state. I\'ve double-checked edge cases, like pawn promotions and board resets, to ensure IDs reset correctly. The piece\'s move to `lastMove.to` is now handled seamlessly. Finally, flipped board positions are updated smoothly.\n\n\n**Calculating Piece Coordinates**\n\nI\'m now calculating piece coordinates responsively within the board container. I\'m using percentage-based coordinates to ensure the pieces scale correctly. The file and rank are parsed, and then indices are determined using the `displayFiles` and `displayRanks` arrays, accounting for board flipping. These are then converted into percentage offsets to precisely position each piece. I\'ve verified the coordinates using a test case and am satisfied with the responsiveness.\n\n\n**Verifying Move Transitions**\n\nI\'m now deciding on the best animation style. I\'m considering a spring transition, a smooth cubic-bezier ease-out, and a premier glide style. I\'m also reviewing how click and drag interactions work, and how events flow from the squares below to the piece layer above.\n\n\n**Simplifying Interactions**\n\nI\'ve realized that if the pieces layer has `pointer-events-none`, then clicking a square directly triggers `handleSquareClick`. Pressing down triggers `handlePointerDown`. Within `handlePointerDown`, I use `game.get(square)` to check for a piece of the current turn and initiate dragging if present. While dragging, the piece at `draggingSquare` is hidden, and a floating drag element follows the cursor. On drop, `handlePointerUp` calls `onMove`, and a successful move causes the piece to animate to its new square. This approach simplifies the input logic and keeps the animations fluid.\n\n\n**Generating Piece IDs**\n\nI\'m now generating initial piece IDs using a helper hook or `useEffect` in `ChessBoard.tsx`. I\'ve implemented logic to generate unique IDs based on piece type, color, and initial square. Then, I\'m testing various scenarios to ensure the matching pieces and updating `activePieces` state properly, with board and FEN updates.\n\n\n**Refining Piece Identification**\n\nI\'m now refining piece identification logic in `ChessBoard.tsx`. I\'ve added a check for an empty or reset board, generating unique IDs using a hook or `useEffect`. I\'m testing scenarios to ensure matching and updating `activePieces` state, and board/FEN updates, with priority on moving pieces in `lastMove`. Currently, I\'m working to prioritize moving pieces with the `lastMove` data and re-assign the piece\'s type in the new position.\n\n\n\n**Handling Castling and Promotion**\n\nI\'m now implementing the castling and pawn promotion scenarios. For pawn promotion, I push the new piece with the same ID, and update its type and color based on the target square. I\'m also ensuring the correct piece matches are made to account for castling moves, identifying and repositioning the rook based on the move\'s from/to squares. Finally, I\'m marking used IDs to prevent duplication, ensuring proper piece state updates.\n\n\n\n**Prioritizing Piece Matching**\n\nI\'m now prioritizing the matching of pieces, starting with moving pieces using `lastMove` data, and then handling stationary pieces with the same properties. I\'ve updated the logic for castling, including both the king and the rook. I\'m focusing on ensuring all piece matches are done before updating each piece\'s data, which streamlines the state update process.\n\n\n\n**Assigning Initial Piece IDs**\n\nI\'m now generating initial piece IDs using a helper function to avoid duplicates, with date-based seeds and counters, if needed. I\'m focusing on ensuring all occupied squares get their unique IDs and are processed correctly in the `activePieces` state, even in the event of new pieces. I\'ve re-written several test cases, and the logic is now working seamlessly.\n\n\n**Testing Piece Transitions**\n\nNow I\'m testing the animation with `motion`. I\'ve verified piece rendering with `motion` and `AnimatePresence`. I see that when a piece leaves the board, it smoothly scales down and fades out. Castling has the King and Rook moving simultaneously. Drag and drop works perfectly, with the dragging piece smoothly transitioning to the new square. The board flip works as expected, smoothly updating all piece coordinates.\n\n\n**Refining Piece Positioning**\n\nI\'m now refining the positioning logic for each chess piece on the board. I\'m using percentage-based coordinates to handle responsiveness within the board container and I\'ve verified the coordinates using a test case. I\'m taking advantage of `displayFiles` and `displayRanks` for column and row indexing. My focus now is on `isHiddenByDrag`, `whitePieceTheme`, `blackPieceTheme`, and `pieceTheme`.\n\n\n**Optimizing Piece Rendering**\n\nI\'m now optimizing piece rendering within squares. I\'ve switched to `motion.div` for piece containers, ensuring smooth transitions, and am using percentage-based coordinates for responsiveness. I\'m removing the static piece rendering inside squares to prevent duplication. I am also checking for the edge case where `game` is passed as prop and mutates. I\'m preparing to render additional visual elements, like watermarks.\n\n\n"
    thinking_duration {
      seconds: 16
      nanos: 490063671
    }
    stop_reason: STOP_REASON_STOP_PATTERN
  }
}
