  planner_response {
    thinking: "**Analyzing Piece Movement**\n\nI\'m currently focused on pinpointing a glitch related to piece movement on the chessboard. My investigation starts with the `handlePointerDown` function within `ChessBoard.tsx`. I\'m meticulously dissecting the sequence of events when a user initiates a piece drag, paying close attention to every detail from that point forward to isolate the source of the issue.\n\n\n**Pinpointing Render Lag**\n\nI\'ve zeroed in on the render lag after a piece drag. It seems the `activePieces` state isn\'t immediately updated during the render phase after `handlePointerUp` executes. Even though `draggingSquare` is null, `activePieces` still shows the piece in its original position for a split second, causing a visual flicker. I need to figure out how to synchronize the state updates.\n\n\n**Isolating Animation Glitch**\n\nMy current focus is on the disconcerting \"snap-back\" glitch. When a piece is dropped, it briefly returns to its origin (`start.square`) before animating to the intended destination (`toSq`). This happens because the state updates, specifically `activePieces`, are not immediately consistent with the rendered position. Additionally, `useEffect` seems to be responsible for the final animation to its destination. This issue appears consistently.\n\nI\'m also considering other scenarios like AI moves, click-to-move, and historical moves. Particularly when viewing move history, changes to the `fen` variable don\'t always align with the animation triggers of `displayGame`, which causes inconsistent visuals. The inconsistencies extend beyond the dragging process. Also I believe the `initial` state on the `motion.div` is a root cause of flickering/glitching issues.\n\n\n**Refining Movement Logic**\n\nI\'m now investigating how other chess applications handle transitions. Drag-and-drop moves should be instantaneous with a visual update upon release, avoiding any unnecessary animation or snap-back. Programmatic moves (AI, opponent, click-to-move) should feature smooth slides, and castling requires simultaneous rook movement. I also need to ensure instantaneous changes when the board is flipped to avoid unwanted visual artifacts.\n\n\n"
    thinking_duration {
      seconds: 4
      nanos: 121941880
    }
    stop_reason: STOP_REASON_STOP_PATTERN
  }
}
