  }
  planner_response {
    thinking: "**Adding Theme Backgrounds**\n\nI\'ve made progress! I\'m now integrating a new theme, `isBatmanTheme`, into `App.tsx`. The goal is to set a wallpaper background for this theme using the user-provided URL, which is `https://www.highreshdwallpapers.com/wp-content/uploads/2015/02/Awesome-Batman-Bat-Symbol.jpg`. I\'ve also set up a fallback mechanism using `https://th.bing.com/`, just in case.\n\n\n**Analyzing Movement Glitch**\n\nI\'m now deeply analyzing the movement glitch. It seems the problem lies in how `setDraggingSquare` immediately unhides a piece before `onMove` updates the game state. Specifically, the \"drag-and-drop drop glitch\" occurs during drag operations when releasing a piece. We want to ensure the piece at square A remains hidden until `onMove` has updated the game state, which is why I\'m now looking at the component lifecycle, and how state is managed!\n\n\n**Refining Piece Animations**\n\nThe current focus is on refining piece animations, particularly after drag-and-drop moves. I\'ve realized that the glitch stems from `activePieces` not updating in sync with the visual rendering. A fix involves immediately updating `activePieces` or keeping the piece hidden until the update, and marking the move as a drag-and-drop to avoid a double animation. Now I\'m considering how to smoothly animate moves initiated by clicks, AI, or online opponents as well. I need the pieces to glide smoothly from the origin to their destination square.\n\n\n**Implementing Piece Animations**\n\nI\'m now implementing spring physics for piece movements! Captured pieces will smoothly fade and scale out. For castling moves, the King and Rook will glide simultaneously to their new squares.\n\nI\'m also tackling board flipping. Animating the entire board with spring when `isFlipped` changes causes chaos. The fix involves tracking `prevFlippedRef` and using a transient flag `isBoardRotating` to immediately update the layout.\n\n\n**Refining Active Piece Synchronization**\n\nI\'m now focusing on a critical synchronization issue. When board states change drastically, outside of a standard chess move, I\'ll instantaneously sync `activePieces` with `transition: { duration: 0 }`. This should prevent any \"flying pieces\" glitches. To ensure glitch-free rendering, I\'m designing a robust reconciliation algorithm for `activePieces`. Each piece has a unique and stable `id`, allowing us to accurately track their movements and removals.\n\n\n**Testing Piece Updates**\n\nI\'m now testing the core game logic involving special moves. I\'ve considered how to handle en passant captures, castling (moving both King and Rook), and pawn promotions. My key insight is that by maintaining piece identity and only updating the square property, the visuals will behave as expected. Moving pieces will smoothly slide, stationary pieces won\'t flicker, captured pieces vanish cleanly, and castling works beautifully. I\'m focusing on validating this within the `game.board()` representation to ensure all updates are correctly reflected.\n\n\n"
    thinking_duration {
      seconds: 5
      nanos: 455913924
    }
    stop_reason: STOP_REASON_STOP_PATTERN
  }
}
