  planner_response {
    thinking: "**Analyzing Move Detection Flaw**\n\nI\'ve zeroed in on a critical flaw in move detection within `ChessBoard`. Specifically, the code incorrectly assumes `lastMove` will always be provided, and that it will be non-null when a move is made. However, at line 381 in `DailyPuzzleView.tsx`, `lastMove` is passed as `null` and it may also be not be provided. This suggests a vulnerability to incorrect FEN transition detections in certain cases. I need to make sure `ChessBoard` doesn\'t exclusively use the \"last move\" to detect such transitions.\n\n\n**Refining Board State Comparison**\n\nI\'ve been further refining the board state comparison approach for move detection. My current focus is on a direct comparison between the `previousBoardState` and the `currentBoardState`. I\'m identifying `vacatedSquares` and `occupiedSquares` to pin-point piece movements. This method handles both normal moves and special moves like castling and en passant effectively.\n\n\n**Optimizing Chessboard Resets**\n\nI\'m now focusing on scenarios where the board is completely reset, such as a new game or puzzle setup, and handling simultaneous piece changes. I need to make sure I reset all pieces to their starting square positions with animation disabled to make the move transition 100% reliable. The mathematical board diffing algorithm appears completely deterministic and is handling all chess scenarios without any external props, like last move, being in sync!\n\n\n**Implementing Move Animations**\n\nI\'m now implementing the move animations, focusing on how pieces transition between squares based on `vacatedSquares` and `occupiedSquares`. For a move like a pawn advancing from `d2` to `d4`, I\'ve made it work great; the transition is smooth! I am also successfully handling en passant, castling, and pawn promotion by making the pieces visibly transition. The drag-and-drop mechanics appear to be working reliably.\n\n\n**Validating Piece Transitions**\n\nI\'m now focusing on validating the piece transitions with the drag-and-drop implementation. My approach is to update the internal board state immediately with user actions, ensuring a seamless experience. When the `onMove` event is triggered, the transition is instant. This eliminates any glitches or redundant slides, solving the drag, snapback, missing-move, and puzzle glitches.\n\n\n**Integrating Batman Theme**\n\nI\'m now integrating the Batman theme into the application. I\'ve added `isBatmanTheme` to `App.tsx` which checks if any theme setting matches \'batman\'. When active, the Gotham Bat-Symbol wallpaper background is applied. I am using a background image URL for this.\n\n\n**Enhancing Batman Theme Integration**\n\nI\'ve just been enhancing the Batman theme, focusing on a more polished implementation. I\'ve added a watermark to `ChessBoard.tsx` displaying the iconic Batman Bat-Symbol. The watermark includes a fallback for image loading failures, ensuring a graceful user experience. I am also using screen overlay to gracefully display the Bat-Symbol on the board.\n\n\n"
    thinking_duration {
      seconds: 5
      nanos: 730235665
    }
    stop_reason: STOP_REASON_STOP_PATTERN
