  planner_response {
    thinking: "**Analyzing ChessBoard.tsx**\n\nI\'m currently focused on dissecting `ChessBoard.tsx`. Specifically, I\'m examining its state management, starting with `activePieces`. I\'m working to understand how piece movement and animation are handled, and what state data underpins it. I\'m seeking to identify potential bottlenecks for clean and glitch-free behavior.\n\n\n**Tracing State Updates**\n\nI\'ve been drilling down into the state updates within `ChessBoard.tsx`, especially when the board changes. I\'m noting how `activePieces` is recomputed based on `game.fen()` or `lastMove` changes, which triggers the building of new board maps (`currBoardMap`). I\'m now comparing these current and previous board maps (`prevBoardMap`) to detect changes via `vacated` and `placed` squares. I\'m aiming to understand the implications of these changes in case A.\n\n\n**Delineating Move Logic**\n\nI\'m now clarifying the move logic for various scenarios. In the case where `placed.length === 0`, no piece has moved, so `activePieces` should remain unchanged, simple! For normal moves, the piece\'s square must be updated in `activePieces`. Captured pieces are removed, and promotions require a `type` update. Furthermore, for a drag-and-drop move, `skipAnimation` flag may be set. I\'m focusing on understanding castling and en passant variations.\n\n\n**Clarifying Pawn Movements**\n\nAlright, I\'ve got a handle on diagonal pawn movements now. A moving pawn updates its square in `activePieces`, and the captured pawn is properly removed. On larger board changes like resets, puzzles, or undo/redo, I\'ll sync `activePieces` directly to `currBoardMap` to ensure a clean match, skipping animation.\n\nFor the visual layer, I have a method for calculating each piece\'s position by finding `colIdx` and `rankIdx` from the piece\'s square. The `motion.div` handles animations, opacity, and scale changes, which allows for visual feedback on drag movements.\n\n\n**Refining Visual Transitions**\n\nI\'m now zeroing in on the visual transitions for the chess pieces. I\'ve realized that setting `initial={false}` is crucial for preventing unwanted animations during the initial page load; this makes pieces appear right away. I\'m carefully examining the drag-and-drop mechanics. Specifically, I\'m noting how `handlePointerDown`, `handlePointerMove`, and `handlePointerUp` work together to trigger the drag state and update the visual layer, especially regarding `opacity`. The key seems to be setting the pieces properly using the updates and `useEffect`.\n\n\n"
    thinking_duration {
      seconds: 4
      nanos: 508676117
    }
    stop_reason: STOP_REASON_STOP_PATTERN
  }
