'm examining the scenario of `prevPieces.length === 0 || Math.abs(prevPieces.length - currentOccupied.length) > 3 && !lastMove`, which indicates a likely full board update, like a game restart or board swap.\n\n\n**Implementing Castling Logic**\n\nI\'m now implementing castling logic within the board reconciliation. I\'m handling the movement of the rook during castling events by finding the relevant rook piece on the board, updating its position accordingly, and ensuring the vacated squares are accounted for. I\'m focusing on ensuring the rook\'s move correctly reflects the game state and reconciles properly within the broader update.\n\n\n**Refining Piece Placement**\n\nI\'m now examining the logic surrounding vacated squares and the placement of new pieces. The current approach checks if exactly one piece has moved and attempts to find a target square in the `unplacedOccupied` array. I\'m focusing on the criteria for identifying the \"moving piece\" and ensuring the correct piece attributes are carried over during the move. The focus is on the criteria for what is considered a \'moving piece,\' to avoid unexpected behavior.\n\n\n**Matching Piece Logic**\n\nI\'m now refining the piece matching logic within the reconciliation. I\'m focusing on the edge cases involving stationary pieces, and ensuring that pieces are correctly matched between board states, considering their square, color, and type. I am concentrating on the loop logic that iterates backward through the `unplacedOccupied` and `prevPieces` arrays, and how pieces are matched according to the different criteria, with particular attention to how I\'m avoiding duplicate matches by using `usedPrevIds`. This seems to be the most critical section for preventing unexpected changes.\n\n\n**Generating Unique IDs**\n\nI\'ve just added logic to generate unique IDs for new pieces spawned during board reconciliation. I\'m using a timestamp and a counter to ensure each piece gets a distinct ID, which will help with tracking and animations. I\'m focusing on incorporating these new IDs into the reconciliation process to maintain a consistent state.\n\n\n"
